+# MODE: brainstorm
+
+## Topic
+Add an MR-style code-quality reviewer to z-harness (a Claude Code plugin of slash commands + subagents that orchestrate planning, implementation, and review of code changes using Claude + cross-LLM consults to Codex and Gemini). The reviewer is distinct from the existing correctness reviewers — it assumes correctness and instead targets the failure modes AI coding agents (Claude included) typically exhibit: excessive defensive error handling for impossible edge cases while missing real ones, repetitive low-signal tests, no abstraction / code length creep, sloppy naming / dead comments / drive-by reformatting, drift from a project STYLE.md. Supports an init subflow that authors STYLE.md interactively (with cross-LLM critique of the draft), can ingest an existing style guide, or accept natural language. Open question: optional add-on after /z-review-all, bundled into /z-review-all as an extra dimension, or standalone command.
+
+## Repo Context
+
+z-harness is a Claude Code plugin with slash commands orchestrating planning/implementation/review cycles. Key agents in `agents/`:
+
+**`auditor.md`** — read-only Sonnet auditor spawned in parallel by `/z-audit`, one per dimension. Dimension is one of: `correctness | perf | cleanliness | design`. Returns structured findings (Location / Evidence / Recommendation / Severity). **Key constraint:** auditor reads an optional rubric file (`rubric_path`); if non-empty, the rubric's checklist **becomes the authoritative scope** for that dimension. Without a rubric, auditor falls back to generic dimension checklists (e.g., cleanliness includes "duplicated logic, dead code, leaky abstractions, layering violations, comments that lie, config sprawl, magic numbers without provenance").
+
+**`codex-reviewer.md`** — post-implementation Haiku agent that calls Codex to scrutinize a just-completed task for bugs, spec violations, missed edge cases, DRY/KISS/SOLID. Runs per-task inside `/z-implement-all` and `/z-implement-next`. Extracts **blockers and majors only** from Codex response; minors/nits dropped per spec budget.
+
+**`codex-consultant.md`, `gemini-consultant.md`** — cross-LLM second opinions during `/z-plan` (Phase 3: bundled-decisions mode) and `/z-review-all` (Phase 7: plan-review mode). Both support a `brainstorm` mode that returns five-section ideation blocks.
+
+## Existing Review Pipeline
+
+**`/z-review-all`** — final-gate Gemini + Codex review after `/z-implement-all` completes. Runs on cumulative diff against SPEC.md to surface (a) implementation drift across tasks, (b) spec gaps that only surface in aggregate. Does NOT currently have a style/quality dimension.
+
+**`/z-audit`** — pre-existing component audit with ≥1 dimension from {correctness, perf, cleanliness, design}. Supports optional rubric file; outputs REPORT.md (all findings) + TASKS.md (curated actionable subset). Runs parallel auditor per dimension. Read-only. **No style guide concept yet. Greenfield.**
+
+**`codex-reviewer`** runs per-task inside implementation phases; enforces blockers+majors only.
+
+## Overlap Risk to Address
+
+The existing `auditor` already supports `cleanliness` and `design` dimensions + optional rubric file. Any new MR-style reviewer must justify why it is not just "`/z-audit --dimension=style --rubric=STYLE.md`". Think through:
+
+- What does MR-style catch that a cleanliness/design auditor misses?
+- Is the difference in *scope* (e.g., "per-diff" vs "per-component"), *feedback cycle* (e.g., live during `/z-review-all` vs pre-flight audit), or *integration point* (e.g., final gate vs decision gate)?
+- Can you express the rubric as a checklist that `auditor` would natively support, or does it need a different agent shape?
+
+## Return exactly five sections: (1) Framing, (2) Core hypothesis, (3) Risks, (4) Plan implications, (5) What would change my mind. Mark any section you cannot produce as `<missing>`. Do not add other sections or a recommendation. Be bold and distinct — diversity across the three ideators is the point.
