+## (1) Framing
+
+This should not be framed as a new “style audit” dimension. The important distinction is **reviewing the delta as an MR**, not auditing the component as a static artifact.
+
+`/z-audit cleanliness/design + rubric` asks: “Is this area good?”
+
+An MR-style reviewer asks: “Did this change make the codebase worse in agent-characteristic ways, even if the feature works?”
+
+That means the unit of review is the diff, surrounding local context, and stated task intent. It should care about:
+
+- avoidable code growth
+- needless abstraction or missing obvious abstraction
+- defensive branches for states that cannot happen
+- missed real boundary cases despite noisy guardrails
+- repetitive tests that increase line count without increasing confidence
+- stale comments and naming drift introduced by the patch
+- unrequested reformatting or churn
+- mismatch with `STYLE.md`
+
+The value is strongest as a **final or near-final gate on changed code**, not as a repo audit.
+
+## (2) Core hypothesis
+
+The reviewer should be a **standalone MR-quality agent with STYLE.md as one input**, then optionally invoked by `/z-review-all`.
+
+Its job is not correctness. It assumes the patch basically works and reviews the human-maintainability cost of accepting it.
+
+The agent shape should differ from `auditor.md`:
+
+- Input should include cumulative diff, touched files, task/spec summary, optional `STYLE.md`, and maybe selected surrounding code.
+- Output should be MR-like: “must fix before merge”, “should fix if cheap”, “ignore”.
+- It should explicitly suppress pure preference nits unless tied to project style, maintainability, or future bug risk.
+- It should look for **agent fingerprints**, not generic cleanliness:
+  - over-broad try/catch
+  - speculative null checks
+  - duplicated test matrices with no semantic variation
+  - comments restating code
+  - new helper names that encode implementation instead of domain meaning
+  - code that grew because the agent avoided editing existing abstractions
+  - unrelated formatting churn
+
+`STYLE.md` is not the reviewer. It is the local constitution the reviewer uses to avoid generic taste.
+
+## (3) Risks
+
+The main risk is creating another reviewer that duplicates existing gates and trains users to ignore review output.
+
+The second risk is style-lawyering. If the reviewer produces low-signal nits, it will be worse than useless because it adds friction after implementation.
+
+The third risk is overfitting `STYLE.md` into a checklist. A good style guide is partly judgment: naming preferences, abstraction thresholds, testing philosophy, error-handling posture. If reduced to mechanical rules, the reviewer becomes a linter with worse determinism.
+
+The fourth risk is final-gate timing. If this only runs after `/z-review-all`, it may find broad quality problems when the implementation context is cold and expensive to fix.
+
+The fifth risk is cross-LLM critique during STYLE.md init becoming too abstract. It needs to produce concrete project rules, not taste essays.
+
+## (4) Plan implications
+
+Add an init subflow first, because the reviewer’s quality depends on having a project-local target.
+
+Likely artifacts:
+
+- `STYLE.md`: human-readable project style guide.
+- Maybe `.z-harness/style-source.md` or metadata if generated from natural language or existing guide.
+- A slash command such as `/z-style-init` or `/z-quality-init`.
+- A reviewer agent such as `mr-quality-reviewer.md`.
+
+The init flow should support three paths:
+
+- ingest existing guide
+- ask interactive questions
+- accept natural-language preferences
+
+Then draft `STYLE.md`, run Codex + Gemini critique in brainstorm or review mode, revise once, and ask for user approval.
+
+For runtime integration, the cleanest shape is:
+
+- standalone `/z-quality-review` for explicit MR-style review of current diff
+- optional `/z-review-all --quality` or config default to include it
+- maybe later bundle it into `/z-review-all` once signal quality is proven
+
+The reviewer should probably emit only blockers and majors by default, with an optional verbose mode for minors.
+
+## (5) What would change my mind
+
+I would collapse this into `/z-audit --dimension=cleanliness --rubric=STYLE.md` if the desired output is mainly a broad codebase/component assessment rather than a diff review.
+
+I would bundle it directly into `/z-review-all` if users treat quality findings as merge-blocking at the same level as correctness drift.
+
+I would make it standalone only if the expected use is iterative: run quality review, clean up the patch, rerun, then proceed to final correctness review.
+
+I would avoid building a new agent if `auditor.md` can be cheaply extended to understand “diff mode” with changed-file context and MR-style output.
+
+I would skip STYLE.md init entirely if the plugin already had a reliable project-doc discovery layer that could synthesize style expectations from existing code, docs, linters, and tests with enough precision.
