+# D2. Multi-LLM dispatch pattern
+**Critique:** Disagree with (b). Go with (a) orchestrated at the CLI level, with a final Sonnet consolidation step.
+
+**Reasoning:** Nesting CLI tool invocations inside a parent agent (Sonnet calling Codex/Gemini) creates a fragile black box. The CLI layer is inherently better at parallel subprocess management, capturing stderr, and handling timeouts.
+
+**Tradeoffs / risks:** With (b), if Codex hangs, Sonnet might timeout or hallucinate a recovery. Furthermore, Sonnet's context window will be heavily taxed if it has to ingest the raw diff plus the raw output of two other models simultaneously.
+
+**Interactions:** Constrains D11. If Sonnet is orchestrating, invoking Opus as a sub-agent from within Sonnet creates a very expensive and slow nested loop.
+
+**Shortcuts:** Relying on the LLM to act as a robust process manager is a shortcut that sacrifices reliability for a cleaner CLI script.
+
+---
+
+# D3. Output format & TASKS.md handoff
+**Critique:** Agree with separate MR-TASKS.md, but disagree with interactive CLI triage.
+
+**Reasoning:** Interactive CLI prompts force synchronous attention and break down if the user needs to pause to investigate a finding in their editor. It also breaks headless CI usage.
+
+**Tradeoffs / risks:** Generating an MR-REVIEW.md with markdown checkboxes (e.g., - [ ] Accept: Extract magic number) allows the user to triage asynchronously. A follow-up command (or /z-implement-all) can parse the checked boxes into the final TASKS.md pipeline.
+
+**Interactions:** Relates to D9. The branch slug provides the persistent directory where this async triage state can live.
+
+---
+
+# D4. /z-style-init Capture mechanism
+**Critique:** Agree with (b) + (a) fallback.
+
+**Reasoning:** Heuristics alone (churn, size) often isolate bloated god-objects or generic utility files rather than structurally sound abstractions. Letting the LLM scan a candidate list yields a much higher signal-to-noise ratio.
+
+**Tradeoffs / risks:** Higher initial token cost and latency, but since this is a one-shot initialization per project, the cost is heavily amortized.
+
+---
+
+# D5. STYLE.md schema
+**Critique:** Agree with (b).
+
+**Reasoning:** Free-form text under strict markdown headers is the sweet spot. Strict YAML fails to capture nuanced engineering philosophy, while pure free-form makes structured citation impossible.
+
+**Interactions:** Directly supports the requirement that reviewers must cite the style guide.
+
+---
+
+# D9. Slug coupling for /z-mr-review
+**Critique:** Agree with (b).
+
+**Reasoning:** Defaulting to git branch --show-current provides zero-config ergonomics for the 90% use case while keeping state cleanly isolated.
+
+**Tradeoffs / risks:** You need a fallback for detached HEAD states or if the user accidentally runs this on main (should probably refuse to run or prompt for a slug on main).
+
+---
+
+# D11. Two-tier model (Sonnet + Opus)
+**Critique:** Disagree. Opus is likely overkill and misapplied here.
+
+**Reasoning:** Finding duplicated abstractions or dead code across a whole repo is primarily a search/RAG problem, not a raw reasoning problem. Opus isn't magically better at finding duplication unless you feed it the entire repository context (prohibitively expensive and slow).
+
+**Tradeoffs / risks:** Using Opus introduces massive latency to a review command that developers expect to be relatively fast.
+
+**Recommendation:** Stick to Sonnet for both, but equip the abstraction-checking pass with robust grep_search or AST tools. Let the tooling do the heavy lifting for whole-repo context.
+
+---
+
+# Missing Decisions
+
+1. **Diff Chunking / Context Limits:** How are massive PRs handled? If a PR touches 50 files, does the orchestrator chunk the git diff per file/module, or throw the whole diff at the agents and hope it fits?
+
+2. **The Feedback Loop (False Positives):** If a user dismisses a finding as a false positive, does the system learn? There needs to be a decision on whether dismissed findings automatically suggest an amendment to STYLE.md so the user doesn't suffer the exact same nitpick on their next PR.
